  abstract = {Personal information tools increasingly offer to import everything a user has: email, files, notes, chats and references. Imports produce duplicates. We collected consented exports from 14 participants across 9 source types and annotated duplicate and near-duplicate pairs. 18\% of imported items had at least one duplicate. Exact hashing found 41\% of duplicate pairs, title-and-size matching 73\%, and content shingling 92\%, but shingling also falsely merged successive versions of drafts that participants wanted kept apart. We propose a two-stage policy: merge exact duplicates silently, group near-duplicates for the user to confirm.},
